    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d781">

                <pb facs="1044" n="1032" sameAs="frus:frus1945v05/pg1032"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.24/8–2145</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Chief of the Division of
                        Lend-Lease and Surplus War Property</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Maxwell</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-08-21">August 21, 1945</date>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lend-Lease Termination</hi>
                </p>
                <p>The Soviet Protocol Committee on August 17 sent out orders to the effect that
                    loadings of Soviet ships in United States ports were to be stopped at once.
                    Loadings of United States ships were also to be stopped and their cargoes
                    discharged. In short, so far as practicable, transfers of lend-lease cargo to
                    the U.S.S.R. were halted at the ports on August 17.</p>
                <p>With respect to other lend-lease countries transfers are to be stopped only as of
                    V–J Day.<anchor n="93" xml:id="d781-f1"/> The outcome is, therefore, that the
                    U.S.S.R. is being treated on a different basis from other countries. <pb
                        facs="1045" n="1033" xml:id="pg1033"/> When I inquired from John
                        Hazard<anchor n="94" xml:id="d781-f2"/> about this, he informed me that the
                    decision was taken deliberately and probably was part of a general squeeze now
                    being put on the U.S.S.R. Certain reasons can, however, be alleged for the
                    discrimination, such as the fact that the U.S.S.R. does not provide us with
                    reverse lend-lease and the fact that the U.S.S.R. does not assist us with
                    redeployment.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d781-f1" n="93" xml:id="d781-f1-content">
                <p>The signing of surrender by Japan took place September 2; for documentation on
                    the Japanese surrender, see <ref target="frus1945v06">vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">vi</hi>
                    </ref>, section under Japan entitled “Surrender of Japan …”, Part I.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d781-f2" n="94" xml:id="d781-f2-content">
                <p>Secretary of the President’s Soviet Protocol Committee.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
